=== layout: subsection

# 30. AMP V. Myriad Genetics

=== layout: one-panel

# 30. Questions

@body

- Discuss the case "Association for Molecular Pathology v. Myriad Genetics". What was the impact of the ruling on Biotechnology?
- What ways are currently used to patent genes?
- How has the AMP v. Myriad Genetics ruling influenced the approach to patenting biotechnological innovations related to human genetics?
- What are the long-term effects of this ruling on patient access to genetic testing and personalized medicine?
- How do current patent laws address the complexity of genetic information and its uses in biotechnology?

=== layout: one-panel

# 30. Finding BRCA Was Not Creating BRCA

@body

- The 2013 Supreme Court decision held that a naturally occurring DNA segment is a product of nature and is not patent eligible merely because it has been isolated.
- The importance and difficulty of locating BRCA1 and BRCA2 did not change that conclusion.
- Pathogenic variants can increase cancer risk; the ordinary biological role of the BRCA genes is not simply to cause cancer.
- Scientific importance and patent eligibility are different judgments.

@citation

https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep569/usrep569576/usrep569576.pdf

=== layout: one-panel

# 30. Why cDNA Was Different

@body

- Complementary DNA is made from an RNA template and can omit introns present in genomic DNA.
- The Court distinguished cDNA that is not naturally occurring, while noting that short sequences without intervening introns may not be distinguishable.
- The decision did not make every laboratory-made molecule automatically patentable.
- Other eligibility questions and the requirements for novelty, nonobviousness, and disclosure still apply.

@citation

https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep569/usrep569576/usrep569576.pdf

=== layout: one-panel

# 30. Protection After Myriad

@body

- Specific engineered constructs, qualifying methods, new compositions, and other inventions can raise different questions from a claim to an isolated natural sequence.
- Diagnostic-method claims also have eligibility limits; do not assume that adding measure a gene makes a natural relationship patentable.
- Discuss access to testing, competing laboratories, and incentives using evidence about the particular setting.
- The ruling did not erase every patent related to genetics or guarantee identical prices and access everywhere.

@citation

https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep569/usrep569576/usrep569576.pdf

=== layout: big-image

@image

![30. Why Genomic DNA and cDNA Differ](assets/set6_drawings/dna_cdna.drawn.svg)

@caption

The colored blocks track exon sequences through a simplified example. Laboratory synthesis alone does not decide patent eligibility.

=== layout: subsection

# 31. CRISPR/Cas9 Patent Disputes

=== layout: one-panel

# 31. Questions (1 of 2)

@body

- Who were Emmanuelle Charpentier and Jennifer Doudna?
- Do you find their story inspiring?
- (Humble brag: Dr. Voss worked in Dr. Doudna's lab for 3 months as a graduate student.)
- Explain the patent wars involving CRISPR technology. How did MIT get their patent approved before Berkeley when they submitted 7 months after them?
- How did the disputed eukaryotic claims differ from the broader CRISPR claims, and why is a simple prokaryote/eukaryote ownership division misleading?
- How did Feng Zhang of the Broad Institute find out about CRISPR/Cas9 to create his patent?

=== layout: one-panel

# 31. Questions (2 of 2)

@body

- What did the 2022 PTAB decision conclude, and what changed after the 2025 appeal?
- How did this decision affect Biotech companies?
- How have the patent wars influenced the development and commercialization of CRISPR?

=== layout: one-panel

# 31. The Biological Discovery and the Tool

@body

- Bacterial CRISPR systems are part of microbial defense. Researchers adapted molecular components into programmable genome-editing tools.
- Charpentier and Doudna's contributions are central to the story; Zhang and others developed important applications in eukaryotic cells.
- The Broad Institute is affiliated with MIT and Harvard; these institutions are not interchangeable names for one patent claim.
- Different claims can cover different systems,\
  components, applications, or settings. Nobel recognition and patent priority answer different questions.

@citation

https://www.cafc.uscourts.gov/opinions-orders/22-1653.OPINION.5-12-2025_2512679.pdf

=== layout: one-panel

# 31. Filing Date and Issue Date Differ

@body

- Filing, examination, issuance, claim scope, and priority disputes are separate events.
- Accelerated examination can affect when a patent issues; issuance order alone does not settle every question of invention priority or overlapping scope.
- The dispute involves how the tool was conceived and applied in eukaryotic cells, not a clean division in which one institution owns all bacteria and the other all eukaryotes.
- Read the actual claims and the decision rather than the headline.

@citation

https://www.cafc.uscourts.gov/opinions-orders/22-1653.OPINION.5-12-2025_2512679.pdf

=== layout: one-panel

# 31. The 2022 Decision Was Not the End

@body

- The 2022 PTAB decision awarded priority to Broad in the interference concerning the disputed eukaryotic CRISPR-Cas9 system.
- On May 12, 2025, the Federal Circuit affirmed part of the decision, vacated the conception determination, and remanded for further proceedings.
- A remand requires further consideration; it is not a declaration that the other side has won every patent.
- Explain these dated decisions and identify unresolved scope. Do not label the 2022 result a final present-day settlement.

@citation

https://www.cafc.uscourts.gov/opinions-orders/22-1653.OPINION.5-12-2025_2512679.pdf

=== layout: one-panel

# 31. What a Startup Needs to Know

@body

- Which editing system, target cell, indication, delivery method, and territory will the company use?
- Which issued claims, licenses, or disputes could affect that use?
- An existing patent does not prove the product works, and a pending dispute does not prove that all research or commercialization has stopped.
- Your scientific milestone and your freedom-to-operate work should remain connected without collapsing into one checkbox.

@citation

https://www.cafc.uscourts.gov/opinions-orders/22-1653.OPINION.5-12-2025_2512679.pdf

=== layout: subsection

# 32. First Inventor to File and Prior Art

=== layout: one-panel

# 32. Questions (1 of 2)

@body

- What is the difference between "First to File" vs. "First to Invent"?
- What is "First Inventor to File"?
- How does each system balance the interests of individual inventors with those of the public?
- What are the potential impacts of the "First Inventor to File" system on small-scale inventors or biotech startups?
- How does prior art influence patentability?
- What is prior art, and how have definitions of prior art and publication changed since the 2013 America Invents Act?

=== layout: one-panel

# 32. Questions (2 of 2)

@body

- What mechanisms exist to invalidate a patent?
- Consider reexamination, challenges based on prior art, or disputes over ownership. How might these laws benefit or harm innovation?

=== layout: one-panel

# 32. It Still Has to Be Your Invention

@body

- The America Invents Act introduced first-inventor-to-file provisions effective March 16, 2013 for the applicable applications and claims.
- This is not permission to take another person's invention and file first. Inventorship and derivation still matter.
- The earlier first-to-invent framework could require evidence about conception and other invention events.
- Current and transitional rules depend on the application's circumstances; do not apply one slogan to every old patent.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/web/offices/pac/mpep/s2159.html

=== layout: one-panel

# 32. Prior Art Can Be More Than a Patent

@body

- Earlier patents, publications, public uses, sales, and other qualifying disclosures can matter under the applicable rules.
- A concept need not have been sold as a working commercial product to appear in prior art.
- Publication can help communicate science while affecting future patent options. U.S. grace-period provisions are limited and are not a worldwide safety net.
- For your company, consider disclosure and filing before publicly sharing a new invention.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/web/offices/pac/mpep/s2159.html

=== layout: one-panel

# 32. How a Patent Can Be Challenged

@body

- An examiner's allowance is not a guarantee that every claim will survive later review.
- Available proceedings can test issues such as earlier disclosures or other requirements, depending on the forum and procedure.
- Distinguish a dispute about who invented something from a challenge to whether a claim is valid.
- Compare the cost and accessibility of challenges with the public interest in removing claims that should not have issued.

@citation

https://www.uspto.gov/web/offices/pac/mpep/s2159.html

